  "reasoning": "The patient presents with constipation, worsening belly pain, and significant fatigue/weakness. These symptoms closely match Reference [1], which describes 'belly pain and constipation' alongside feeling 'very weak' and concludes with a diagnosis of typhoid. Reference [6] similarly links abdominal pain, bowel changes (constipation/diarrhea), and persistent tiredness to typhoid. Other potential diagnoses are ruled out by the absence of their hallmark symptoms: peptic ulcer disease typically involves burning stomach pain triggered by food; gastroesophageal reflux disease presents with burping and chest discomfort; urinary tract infection features abnormal urination; diabetes involves excessive thirst and frequent urination; and migraines center on headaches and visual disturbances. The combination of gastrointestinal distress and profound weakness strongly establishes typhoid as the correct diagnosis.",
